{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788305435,"nonce":"cpca5pEE6to27xiyZkxhJWZlOnU=","message":"FIXED, AND THE DISCRIMINATOR IS THE GUARD. Branch `fix/s21-suite-failures`, commit `4eae60d`, on top of `939f92e`. **Not run against a bench**; builds clean in Release, no assembly on the branch.\n\n## The fix, exactly as the Acceptance section asks\n\n    bool owedJournalLine = (repaired.Count \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 !dryRun) || stillUnder.Count \u003e 0;\n    long seq = owedJournalLine ? Act(…) : 0;\n    …\n    [\"action\"] = owedJournalLine ? Stamp(seq) : NoStamp(),\n\n**Keyed on the emit GUARD, never on the returned seq.** That is the half that matters and it is worth saying why out loud: `Journal.Emit` really can return 0 when the writer really is closed, so a fix keyed on `seq == 0` would have swapped one wrong answer for another — it would have silenced the warning in exactly the case the warning exists for. 0 was a **sentinel doing double duty**: \"nothing was owed\" and \"something was owed and the write failed\" shared a value. That is the same defect as `enabled_but_incapable`'s absent key (fixed in `939f92e`, where an absent key meant both \"nobody is impaired\" and \"this build does not publish that\"), one level down and wearing different clothes.\n\nSo a clean dry run now publishes `{\"journal_seq\": null, \"provenance\": \"not applicable — nothing was mutated\"}`, and a genuine emit failure still gets `Stamp(0)`'s warning.\n\n## The suite, promoted from FINDING 5.2c\n\n- **5.2c** — the key exists (`shape()` first, because `eq(..., None)` passes on an absent key).\n- **5.2c2** — the seq is `null` and not `0`, asserted as the thing that distinguishes the two states rather than as a cosmetic value.\n- **5.2c3** — the sentence beside it does not claim the writer is closed or that the session is unprovenanced.\n- **7.2p8** — **the other side of the guard**, which is the half that makes this a real check rather than a one-way assertion: phase 7's dry run falls SHORT of the floor, so `stillUnder \u003e 0`, so it genuinely DOES owe a journal line and must publish an integer seq. Without it, \"always emit null on a dry run\" would pass. It is also the only state in which the `\"plan\"` step name is reachable — the side note in this issue, now exercised.\n- **9.11c** — re-derives the guard out of `PawnManageVerbs.cs` offline, so a future edit cannot quietly key it on the seq again.\n- **9.6w** — still asserts the DEFECT on `12-workcover-dryrun.json`, banked from `1.0.0+52606d1` before the fix. Kept rather than re-banked: it is the evidence this issue was filed from, and rewriting it would destroy the only record of what was wrong.\n\n## The class, recorded in DESIGN\n\nAll three of this round's fixes are one entry in the decisions log, because they are one defect in three places: `work_coverage.order` described an ordering the rows did not have (`9b179ef`), a dry run's `coverage_after` described a state that was not after anything (`58794e4`), and `Stamp(0)`'s sentence described a journal writer that was not closed (this one). **A field that states a false reason is the same defect as a read that hides the truth** — which is `61794cd` (BloodLoss truncated out of a health read) and `7382bdd` (a dropped argument reporting success) one size up. Prose shipped INSIDE an envelope reads as authoritative, which is what makes it worse than an absent field: it invites exactly the shortcut it breaks. This one is the sharpest of the three, because the false sentence is an instruction — \"treat anything done in this session as unprovenanced\" — issued by the one call in the surface guaranteed to have changed nothing.\n\n## Re-run\n\nFull sweep on a fresh `--quicktest` map **after a Release rebuild**. Expect 5.2c/5.2c2/5.2c3 PASS with `journal_seq: null`, and 7.2p8 PASS with an integer seq ≥ 1 on the short-by-two dry run. `--selftest` is 75 checks, exit 0.","files":null}]}